-- 🚨 REGRESSION CELL — the receiver-identity narrowing must NOT trust a lone
-- surviving candidate when the receiver's TYPE has another named-field constructor
-- that withholds the field.  (`resolveFieldOwnersNarrowed` / `narrowedPick` /
-- `narrowingBlockedByCtorSibling`, compiler/types/typecheck.mdk.)
--
-- `mkOth.x` projects a field that only the receiver's SIBLING constructor `Zed`
-- declares.  A value of type `T` carries no static constructor tag, so the
-- receiver's type cannot say whether `Zed`'s layout applies — and the narrowing's
-- comparator compares RESULT-TYPE heads, so it lands on exactly one survivor
-- (`Zed`) for ANY receiver of type `T`.
--
-- MEASURED on the unrepaired slice `ada2bd57`: `check` exit 0 with ZERO
-- diagnostics, and the BUILT BINARY exit 0 printing `99` — the sibling
-- constructor's field, i.e. silent wrongness on the emit path, where base
-- `d2b7cb8b` had rejected the program on check/run/build alike.  The repair
-- restores the rejection pinned below.
--
-- PAIR: this cell alone does not test the property.  Its control is
-- `record_ctor_siblings_all_declare/` (both constructors declare the field ⇒ two
-- survivors ⇒ the narrowing already fell through, so that cell must not move), and
-- the ACCEPT cell proving the repair did not simply disable the narrowing is
-- `test/llvm_fixtures/record_owner_narrowing_flat.mdk`.
import m1.{T(..), mkOth}
import m2.{U(..), mkU}

main = println mkOth.x
